WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.205 --> 00:00:01.330
DEREK DOWNEY: Hi, everyone.

00:00:01.330 --> 00:00:04.110
I'm Derek Downey, a
developer advocate at Google.

00:00:04.110 --> 00:00:05.760
I want to show
you how easy it is

00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:09.090
to get started developing on
Cloud Spanner, Google's highly

00:00:09.090 --> 00:00:10.950
scalable relational database.

00:00:10.950 --> 00:00:12.505
Over the course
of several videos,

00:00:12.505 --> 00:00:14.880
you will learn how to set up
your development environment

00:00:14.880 --> 00:00:16.320
to work with Spanner.

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:19.290
You will see how to set up a
Spanner instance and database,

00:00:19.290 --> 00:00:22.170
as well as details on
defining your schema.

00:00:22.170 --> 00:00:24.870
And finally, you will
learn how to read and write

00:00:24.870 --> 00:00:28.860
your data to Spanner instances
using standard SQL transactions

00:00:28.860 --> 00:00:30.660
and Spanner mutations.

00:00:30.660 --> 00:00:32.310
In this first quick
start video, I

00:00:32.310 --> 00:00:34.410
will connect a simple
Java application

00:00:34.410 --> 00:00:36.360
to the Cloud Spanner emulator.

00:00:36.360 --> 00:00:39.240
The emulator allows you to
mimic Spanner functionality

00:00:39.240 --> 00:00:41.550
on your local
development machine.

00:00:41.550 --> 00:00:43.230
This quick start
will also highlight

00:00:43.230 --> 00:00:45.810
creating the Spanner
schema, as well as reading

00:00:45.810 --> 00:00:47.610
and writing data into Spanner.

00:00:47.610 --> 00:00:50.250
This won't be a typical
"Hello, world" application.

00:00:50.250 --> 00:00:53.620
I'm going to build the start
of a simple banking app.

00:00:53.620 --> 00:00:58.110
This app will allow customers
to create an account and log in.

00:00:58.110 --> 00:01:01.100
I will use the
JetBrains IntelliJ IDE.

00:01:01.100 --> 00:01:03.590
If you don't use
IntelliJ, don't worry.

00:01:03.590 --> 00:01:05.209
You can still get
started quickly

00:01:05.209 --> 00:01:07.170
using your IDE of choice.

00:01:07.170 --> 00:01:08.270
So let's get to it.

00:01:13.220 --> 00:01:15.910
I will be connecting into
Cloud Spanner in this banking

00:01:15.910 --> 00:01:17.450
application.

00:01:17.450 --> 00:01:19.370
However, since I'm
just getting started,

00:01:19.370 --> 00:01:22.480
I want to use the Cloud Spanner
emulator, which runs directly

00:01:22.480 --> 00:01:24.300
on my machine.

00:01:24.300 --> 00:01:26.730
The Spanner emulator
mimics the functionality

00:01:26.730 --> 00:01:28.290
of a Spanner instance.

00:01:28.290 --> 00:01:30.450
I can switch to a
real Spanner instance

00:01:30.450 --> 00:01:34.440
later when this application
is ready for collaboration.

00:01:34.440 --> 00:01:37.520
So the first thing I need to do
is install the gcloud command

00:01:37.520 --> 00:01:38.630
line tool.

00:01:38.630 --> 00:01:40.820
There are instructions
in the video description

00:01:40.820 --> 00:01:43.890
of how to do this for
your operating system.

00:01:43.890 --> 00:01:47.660
But once gcloud is installed, I
will create a new configuration

00:01:47.660 --> 00:01:49.770
for our banking project.

00:01:49.770 --> 00:01:51.660
The project I
specified doesn't have

00:01:51.660 --> 00:01:54.960
to be a real Google Cloud
project, since the emulator

00:01:54.960 --> 00:01:56.280
won't validate it.

00:01:56.280 --> 00:01:58.860
But this is needed
for our application

00:01:58.860 --> 00:02:00.960
to use Spanner's Java library.

00:02:00.960 --> 00:02:02.010
All right.

00:02:02.010 --> 00:02:06.210
Now I will start the emulator
as a background task.

00:02:06.210 --> 00:02:08.940
Once that is running, I will
create an instance called

00:02:08.940 --> 00:02:09.990
"banking."

00:02:09.990 --> 00:02:12.270
This uses the emulator
configuration,

00:02:12.270 --> 00:02:14.460
since we are using the emulator.

00:02:14.460 --> 00:02:17.220
Now, we have our Spanner
instance running.

00:02:17.220 --> 00:02:20.970
But it doesn't do any good
without a database and schema.

00:02:20.970 --> 00:02:22.980
I could create this
in the command line.

00:02:22.980 --> 00:02:25.350
But I want to make sure
this step is repeatable,

00:02:25.350 --> 00:02:27.930
since I will eventually
move this to a real Spanner

00:02:27.930 --> 00:02:29.230
instance.

00:02:29.230 --> 00:02:33.150
So I have created a file that
has the DDL commands to create

00:02:33.150 --> 00:02:34.920
the database and my table.

00:02:34.920 --> 00:02:37.800
For now, I am creating
a single customers table

00:02:37.800 --> 00:02:40.630
that stores some basic
information about the user,

00:02:40.630 --> 00:02:44.550
including a username and
password for authentication.

00:02:44.550 --> 00:02:47.580
Once I've provided the instance
and database configuration,

00:02:47.580 --> 00:02:51.210
I can run the file from my IDE.

00:02:51.210 --> 00:02:51.750
Great.

00:02:51.750 --> 00:02:56.530
Now I have the database
defined with a customers table.

00:02:56.530 --> 00:02:59.740
One neat thing is the ability
to explore the database directly

00:02:59.740 --> 00:03:01.510
within the IDE.

00:03:01.510 --> 00:03:05.380
To do this in IntelliJ, I need
to add the Cloud Spanner data

00:03:05.380 --> 00:03:08.110
source from the database tools.

00:03:08.110 --> 00:03:10.840
With that added, I need
to populate the instance

00:03:10.840 --> 00:03:12.550
and project information.

00:03:12.550 --> 00:03:14.500
Since I'm using the
emulator, I don't

00:03:14.500 --> 00:03:16.690
need to authenticate
to the instance.

00:03:16.690 --> 00:03:19.720
But I will need the URL to
connect to the local host

00:03:19.720 --> 00:03:24.230
on port 9010, which is the
default for the emulator.

00:03:24.230 --> 00:03:30.240
I'll also pass in the autoconfig
emulator equals true parameter.

00:03:30.240 --> 00:03:30.900
Perfect.

00:03:30.900 --> 00:03:33.570
I could see my schema
that I created previously.

00:03:33.570 --> 00:03:35.460
I'm almost done.

00:03:35.460 --> 00:03:37.470
To finish up, I want
to write some data

00:03:37.470 --> 00:03:39.750
and then read it back.

00:03:39.750 --> 00:03:42.780
I'll need to be able to have
users sign up to my bank.

00:03:42.780 --> 00:03:46.890
And I do that in my
createCustomer function.

00:03:46.890 --> 00:03:50.670
I am using standard SQL within
a read/write transaction

00:03:50.670 --> 00:03:54.210
to insert a user into the
customers table based on input

00:03:54.210 --> 00:03:56.810
provided to the application.

00:03:56.810 --> 00:04:00.230
Once a customer is signed up,
then they will need to log in.

00:04:00.230 --> 00:04:03.140
The login function will
issue a read from Spanner

00:04:03.140 --> 00:04:06.380
to validate the
user's credentials.

00:04:06.380 --> 00:04:09.440
With the ability for customers
to sign up and log in,

00:04:09.440 --> 00:04:11.810
I am ready to run
the application.

00:04:11.810 --> 00:04:14.870
I tell the application
that I want to sign up.

00:04:14.870 --> 00:04:18.589
And then I provide
my information.

00:04:18.589 --> 00:04:21.350
Once my customer is
created, I can log in

00:04:21.350 --> 00:04:25.270
by providing username
and password.

00:04:25.270 --> 00:04:28.210
And I've successfully logged in.

00:04:28.210 --> 00:04:30.400
Back in my editor, I
can see the new user

00:04:30.400 --> 00:04:33.550
that was entered
from the application.

00:04:33.550 --> 00:04:34.570
Fantastic.

00:04:34.570 --> 00:04:37.570
It didn't take long to get my
simple application interacting

00:04:37.570 --> 00:04:39.490
with the Spanner emulator.

00:04:39.490 --> 00:04:41.920
This is great for getting
my feet wet with Spanner.

00:04:41.920 --> 00:04:43.990
But soon, I will
need other developers

00:04:43.990 --> 00:04:45.920
to collaborate on
this application.

00:04:45.920 --> 00:04:48.430
And for that, I will need
to create an actual Spanner

00:04:48.430 --> 00:04:49.600
instance.

00:04:49.600 --> 00:04:51.370
In the next video,
I will go over

00:04:51.370 --> 00:04:54.400
what a Spanner instance is
and the different options

00:04:54.400 --> 00:04:55.510
for creating one.

00:04:55.510 --> 00:04:58.180
In the meantime, go ahead and
try out the Spanner emulator

00:04:58.180 --> 00:04:59.050
for yourself.

00:04:59.050 --> 00:05:01.720
And let us know in the
comments your experience.

00:05:01.720 --> 00:05:04.770
[MUSIC PLAYING]

